      <description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;21-converting-the-storyboard-to-code&#34;&gt;2.1. Converting the storyboard to code&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#21-converting-the-storyboard-to-code&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So, let&amp;rsquo;s convert the Storyboard to Swift code!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The following image is how the grid on the device screen is defined.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img src=&#34;//localhost:1313/gitbook-assets/grid (1) (1).png&#34; alt=&#34;&#34;&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;&lt;p&gt;Grid of a screen&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img src=&#34;//localhost:1313/gitbook-assets/Screenshot 2023-05-09 at 10.50.48 PM (2).png&#34; alt=&#34;&#34; width=&#34;375&#34;&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;&lt;p&gt;App1 design&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In the above image, we can see how the UI elements are placed on the device&amp;rsquo;s screen in our App1.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;We will add the code directly to ViewController.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
